0 years ago  that the parties are largely ideological  parties which is to say that the most  conservative democrat is to the left of  the most liberal republican at this  point and that really was not the case  for better or worse in the 20th century  kind of the middle part of the 20th  century so I think that the moderates  have been squeezed out well I don't know  I mean in part it is that moderates have  been squeezed out it is simply also that  the parties themselves have become more  ideologically uniform so for instance  you know in the 1950s for much of the  20th century really till the civil  rights movement you know you had a very  powerful conservative southern block  that was in the Democratic Party you  also had a kind of string of liberal  Republicans it's less of a block but  certainly a liberal Republican tradition  and since then partly as the South has  shifted into the Republican Party and  has become a red area instead of the  solid blue area that it was for much of  the 20th century  you know you've seen the the flavor and  the character of the parties change  right in the sense that they are more  ideologically driven they are less  region-based though of course ideology  and region sort of go go hand in hand in  certain ways so I think there are a lot  of structural developments like that  that aren't talked about as much another  one is the use of the primary election  which has been around for a long time  was around for most of the 20th century  but really only gained the currency that  it has today beginning in the 1970s and  primaries are good for any number of  reasons right and they were sort of  brought about as a way to democratize  the political panel was that well so  primaries were sort of a progressive era  idea and so a lot of primaries started  in the early 20th century but it's  really in the aftermath of the 1968  Democratic convention and and other  things that happened in the 60s and 70s  that you begin to get primaries being  really the chief way in which parties  put the candidates as opposed to  conventions as opposed to conventions as  opposed to some blend of primaries and  conventions you had a much more mixed  process and so that tends to drive  candidates that appeal to the party's  base right which tends to also drive a  certain amount of ideological division  so I think there are are we living in an  era of wonderful bipartisan cooperation  no is it as bad as it has ever been  probably not that either but I think the  really key thing is that there are a lot  of reasons for this that go beyond sort  of what people feel in their hearts and  and the ways in which we use our  political language I think there are a  lot of important structural things to  talk about when you teach about the  presidency are there are there certain  main points that you really want your  students to to leave with what is it  about the office of the presidency that  you like to convey you think it's  important to convey well to some degree  I think what I like to convey are some  of the limits on presidential power  which is to say that I often find myself  as a history  Korean journalists even the public as a  whole presidents make for very neat and  easy narratives right you go from this  person to this person you have a  struggle there are two sides something  results and then you have a single  figure in which to to kind of look at  the ways in which political shifts  happen you know it could be argued that  we devote an inordinately say  congressional elections to other areas  of court justice appointments or even  people who exercise enormous power in  the government who are not elected at  all so I think it's worth thinking both  about some of the limits on the  presidency and also at the ways in which  the presidency really has changed a  tremendous amount over time people's  expectations of what a president is  going to do what he is going to be  responsible for how they themselves  relate 