lk a little bit about the  the difference in the approach that a  historian takes towards well let's call  it the recent pass but nonetheless the  past versus say political science or  journalists who were very much who I  guess they could also study the past or  but but are typically focused on the the  present and watching things unspool well  I'd say the first thing is that  historians spend a lot of time talking  about you know when is something  officially history and by history we  don't mean yesterday right what that  tends to mean is when do we have  sufficient distance on events to begin  to take the long view and that is  roughly say 30 years that's often where  it kind of falls out so right now  historians are very interested in the  1970s and 1980s and they're a handful of  people who are doing more recent things  but you know I'd say roughly think I  don't know what does this election mean  ask me in 30 years and that I will be  able to begin to have an answer there  but the other reason that that's  particularly important to you historians  is that historians like to go to  archives historians like to go to  archives have papers be able to tell the  inside story based on the record that is  left behind now that doesn't mean  historians don't crunch numbers or do  interviews they do all of those things  as well but  and particularly when you're talking  about government there's a kind of lag  time in when you can find out what was  really going on so a lot of the ways in  which historians think about these sorts  of questions and what becomes history  are driven by again this is particularly  government but not exclusive to  government you know when are these  sources going to open up when are they  going to be available when can we both  have distance and find out what's really  going on exactly let's talk about  candidates pedigrees for a second  especially since the first Bush  presidency the number of the main  candidates the ultimate candidates for  president the parties candidates have  had what you might call elite pedigrees  they went to great schools like Yale and  Harvard why don't we see more populist  candidates with BIOS that are somewhat  biographies are somewhat more  representative of the American  electorate that's a really interesting  question I mean we should note this is a  devastating year for Yale right so we've  got to to Harvard candidates out there  and you know I would say it's not wholly  unusual unfortunately overall Harvard  presidents have tended to stack up a  little better in my estimation of Yale  presidents which is to say you know  Harvard can claim Theodore Roosevelt  Franklin Roosevelt John F Kennedy and  Yale has Taft and of course Clinton ed  some of the more recent Bush  presidencies as well both Bush  presidencies but I think it is an  interesting and important question I  mean both about sort of why is this  happening now but also how does this  change the kinds of ways that people  relate to the political system I mean it  is as I said important to note that it's  rather unusual to have right the  populist candidates have always been in  the minority which is to say when you  think particularly about the 20th  century I mean certainly there's been  Lyndon Johnson we had Harry Truman  though we kind of had Harry Truman by  accident we also kind of had Lyndon  Johnson by accident and then you have  Richard Nixon probably as  the most humble candidate elected in his  own right well he was a Duke graduate  right but he nonetheless I would say  both didn't come out of quite the same  elite world and was actually someone who  was able to channel a kind of resentment  against elites as part of his  presidential personality his campaign  appeal and such um so I think I mean I  don't have I suppose a full explanation  as to why this is the case but I think  it deserves a lot more discussion and a  lot more scrutiny than it's actually  gotten I mean both the question of  pedigree 