tion has  to do with what everybody now calls  including the president Obama care his  health reform Act is it true that there  are present loopholes that give big  companies the right to dump their health  insurance policies and basically send  everyone straight into Obamacare yeah  you know I can see where that question  is coming you know actually because  there's a lot of talk about this sort of  thing but I couldn't tell you yeah the  technicalities on on that particular  question but that that worry yeah and  that concern is certainly something that  you see floating around a lot but  whether or not it has any basis I just I  don't really know my impulse is that I  don't think that it does but I have not  read that okay something for future  research um let's talk about parties  again for some political parties we're  essentially a two-party system in this  country I wonder do you think that  assuming adequate assuming not  adequately major financial resources but  we likely to see a serious run by a  third party candidate in our lifetimes  well the role of third parties is very  interesting I mean so the Republicans  are the most successful third will  really fourth party in terms of their  founding right they're the only one  that's really lasted the party of  Lincoln right which comes into being in  the 1850s and 1860s up but those were  rather particular circumstances Civil  War slavery sectional division so I mean  in terms of modern American politics by  which I mean the 20th century there have  been real moments where third party and  sometimes even fourth party candidates  made  pretty significant difference so we  mentioned that we're at the hundredth  anniversary of the 1912 election one of  the things that made that election so  interesting is that there were actually  four major serious candidates who each  got pretty substantial proportions of  the votes so there was Woodrow Wilson  running as a Democrat William Howard  Taft the incumbent president running as  the Republican though he comes in third  you have Teddy Roosevelt who is running  on the Progressive Party ticket and you  have Eugene Debs who is running on the  Socialist Party ticket in that moment  the Socialists get 6% of the vote they  don't win any electoral votes but it's  not in substantial proportion of the  vote Teddy Roosevelt of course gets a  much more substantial proportion comes  in second and Woodrow Wilson takes that  election with a plurality so I'd say the  story of third parties and you can see  this in other moments too 1948 is  another famous four party election but  what they tend to do is first of all  shape the particular election in which  they are involved they tend not to last  and the other interesting thing is that  they tend to be markers of a kind of  transitional moment in the structure of  the two existing parties so you look at  a moment like 1948 in which you have a  Progressive Party candidate who is Henry  Wallace you have Truman as the Democrat  you have wait who's the Republican in  1948 why am i forget doing right so  doing the Republican in 1948 then you  have Strom Thurmond running on a states  rights party within sort of a faction of  the Democratic Party but many people  look at that and in the long view see  that as the beginning of the kind of  transition of the white South out of the  Democratic Party into the Republican  Party and it's a process that takes  place over 30 years again a similar  story told it's George Wallace's  candidacies so I think those are the  roles that third parties tend to play at  this point in American politics you're  absolutely right it would take really a  tremendous amount of money to start out  making a run at the presidential level  there of course many other levels at  which third parties can play a pretty  significant role but so it does seem  like in  our moment the most likely scenario is a  very very rich guy deciding he's gonna  make a go of it for the presidency  s