ates have always been rather limited  in a whole variety of ways so that maybe  is not a word that we that we  necessarily want to hold on to or choose  it's very complicated word but in terms  of where we're going I mean I guess I  would have to fall back on my role as a  historian and say I don't know exactly I  mean we will see what happens  with this election and I think there is  really a sense in many ways that the  machinery of government has broken down  in significant ways and so I wouldn't be  surprised if we're actually on the cusp  of a period of reform of political  institutions which is to say as we were  talking about this question of  bipartisanship and the kinds of  deadlocks we've been seeing so much of  them have to do with particular  structures of the parties at this moment  even the ways that congressional  committees tend to work right now so I  wouldn't be surprised if in that limited  way we're on the verge of a real push  for some kind of structural change there  there still seems to me on a lot of the  biggest questions to be an awful lot of  consensus in the United States and this  has always been one of the one of the  real tensions of American history are we  a country of deep conflict or are we a  country in which a lot of the big  questions have always been pretty  settled and the range of political  debate is actually quite small and I  think we're at a moment when those  questions are coming to the fore again  any special plans for watching tonight's  debate no but I am gonna do it yeah my  my husband is a political cartoonist so  he's always very entertaining to watch  so he's often you know deep in the  minutiae and and and watching for the  moments of humor and energy so will he  be sketching but he's but he's an  entertaining partner to have a debate  good  well Beverly gage thank you so much for  joining us and thanks to all of you for  watching at Yale live I hope that you'll  join us again next time Eric Gershon at  Yale live